From: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:19:57 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 12/23] [mya] Map whole 1 MB windows of a snapshot's memory,
 and keep recent mappings Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Corpse::Memory mapped exactly the pages each read asked for and unmapped a
Region as soon as its last reader released it. A heap walk reads millions of
small values, most of them near the last one read, so nearly every read cost
a mach_vm_remap and a later munmap.

Now:
- A read that fits in an aligned window of mappingWindowSize (1 MB) maps the
  whole window, and later reads in it are served from that Region.
- Memory keeps the most recently mapped Regions (4096 on Darwin) mapped after
  their last reader releases them, in a ring; keepRecentMappings(n) changes
  the bound, and 0 restores the old behaviour.
- A window that cannot be mapped whole (it holds a hole or an unreadable
  page) is remembered, and reads in it map their own pages as before. A
  single page that cannot be mapped is remembered with its error, since a
  walk meets the same unmappable page many times along an array or table.

Reads that do not fit in one window are mapped as before.

The Memory suite gains a window arena and checks that a read maps its whole
window, that the window outlives its readers, that a read across two windows
maps its own pages, that the bound releases the oldest, and that an
unmappable page is remembered with its error. The original arena is now
allocated at a window start, so that its hole keeps reads in it page by page.

